1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:02,107
Hey there, and welcome!

2
00:00:02,147 --> 00:00:07,385
Today you are going to lead a team of
friendly robot helpers through a real,

3
00:00:07,425 --> 00:00:08,357
messy problem.

4
00:00:08,397 --> 00:00:12,871
And you will learn why it works, not just
which buttons to press.

5
00:00:13,111 --> 00:00:16,563
This is your quick start guide for workshop
two.

6
00:00:16,603 --> 00:00:18,527
Grab a snack, and let's go!

7
00:00:19,925 --> 00:00:20,956
First, meet Sam.

8
00:00:20,996 --> 00:00:25,709
Sam is the support lead at a little online
shop called Harbor and Pine.

9
00:00:25,749 --> 00:00:30,328
It is Monday morning, ten minutes before the
big ten o'clock meeting,

10
00:00:30,368 --> 00:00:33,340
and the inbox just exploded with bug
reports.

11
00:00:33,580 --> 00:00:37,903
Sam has one question to answer: what should
the engineers fix first?

12
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:43,943
To help Sam, there is a tiny website called
the Issue Triage Board.

13
00:00:43,983 --> 00:00:49,535
It shows eight pretend bug reports, each
with a title, a category, and a status.

14
00:00:49,575 --> 00:00:54,218
It is small on purpose: no database, nothing
to install, just Node.

15
00:00:54,458 --> 00:00:57,245
But the board is unfinished, on purpose!

16
00:00:57,285 --> 00:00:58,942
Four things are missing.

17
00:00:58,982 --> 00:01:03,182
The priority badges all say untriaged, so
nothing is ranked.

18
00:01:03,222 --> 00:01:05,161
The search box does nothing.

19
00:01:05,201 --> 00:01:08,907
The summary says detailed counts unlock in
lab three.

20
00:01:09,147 --> 00:01:12,012
And there is no export button at all.

21
00:01:13,425 --> 00:01:15,112
So why does that matter?

22
00:01:15,152 --> 00:01:17,126
Without it, Sam is guessing.

23
00:01:17,166 --> 00:01:20,579
With it, Sam can say: here are the top
problems,

24
00:01:20,619 --> 00:01:24,104
here is the proof, and here is what to fix
first.

25
00:01:24,144 --> 00:01:26,838
Better answers, in minutes, not hours.

26
00:01:28,258 --> 00:01:29,810
So how do we build it?

27
00:01:29,850 --> 00:01:32,848
Not with one giant robot doing everything.

28
00:01:32,888 --> 00:01:33,717
With a team!

29
00:01:33,757 --> 00:01:36,249
A coordinator robot makes the plan.

30
00:01:36,289 --> 00:01:42,036
A reviewer robot only reads and checks, and
it is not allowed to change a thing.

31
00:01:42,276 --> 00:01:45,588
And builder robots each write one small
piece.

32
00:01:45,628 --> 00:01:49,304
Specialists usually beat one overloaded
generalist.

33
00:01:50,717 --> 00:01:53,131
Three words you will hear all day.

34
00:01:53,171 --> 00:01:56,306
An agent is a helper with a job description:

35
00:01:56,346 --> 00:02:00,132
its role, its instructions, and the tools it
may use.

36
00:02:00,372 --> 00:02:05,440
A skill is a recipe card of know-how that a
helper can read when needed.

37
00:02:05,480 --> 00:02:08,703
A recipe card gives knowledge, not new
powers.

38
00:02:08,943 --> 00:02:14,114
And a subagent is a helper that your main
agent hands a small job to,

39
00:02:14,154 --> 00:02:18,720
working in its own fresh context so the main
chat stays tidy.

40
00:02:20,133 --> 00:02:21,654
Now for the tricky part.

41
00:02:21,694 --> 00:02:26,532
What happens when several robots work in the
same project at the same time?

42
00:02:26,572 --> 00:02:26,972
Chaos!

43
00:02:26,962 --> 00:02:31,735
They overwrite each other's work, and nobody
knows whose version is right.

44
00:02:33,133 --> 00:02:34,372
The fix is lanes.

45
00:02:34,412 --> 00:02:38,885
Each robot owns its own files, and nobody
else touches them.

46
00:02:38,925 --> 00:02:42,269
Shared rules, which we call frozen
contracts,

47
00:02:42,309 --> 00:02:47,459
are agreed before anyone starts, so everyone
builds to the same plan.

48
00:02:47,699 --> 00:02:50,714
Each robot can even work in its own
worktree,

49
00:02:50,754 --> 00:02:54,517
which is a separate folder checkout of the
same project.

50
00:02:54,557 --> 00:02:58,930
And one integrator robot puts all the pieces
together at the end.

51
00:02:59,170 --> 00:03:02,460
One honest warning: worktrees separate
files,

52
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:07,861
but not ports or secrets, so two apps can
still fight over the same port.

53
00:03:09,258 --> 00:03:12,425
Here is how the board decides what is
urgent.

54
00:03:12,465 --> 00:03:15,276
Take issue one oh one, the checkout bug.

55
00:03:15,316 --> 00:03:17,698
High impact is worth sixty points.

56
00:03:17,738 --> 00:03:20,121
It is blocked, so add twenty five.

57
00:03:20,161 --> 00:03:23,399
A hundred or more users are affected, add
ten.

58
00:03:23,639 --> 00:03:26,455
It has waited a week or more, add five.

59
00:03:26,495 --> 00:03:30,848
Sixty plus twenty five plus ten plus five
makes one hundred.

60
00:03:30,888 --> 00:03:34,509
And eighty or above is priority one: fix it
first!

61
00:03:35,925 --> 00:03:41,037
The rest of today is four hands-on labs, a
hundred and twenty minutes in all.

62
00:03:41,077 --> 00:03:46,389
Lab one, thirty minutes: set the team's
rules, and build a reviewer and a skill.

63
00:03:46,429 --> 00:03:50,068
Lab two, thirty five minutes: build the
priority score.

64
00:03:50,308 --> 00:03:55,042
Lab three, forty minutes: add search and the
summary at the same time.

65
00:03:55,082 --> 00:03:57,905
Lab four, fifteen minutes: add the export,

66
00:03:57,945 --> 00:04:01,246
run the final checks, and write an honest
report.

67
00:04:02,675 --> 00:04:08,953
Every step in the lab guides follows the
same pattern, so you never feel lost.

68
00:04:08,993 --> 00:04:09,844
What to do.

69
00:04:09,884 --> 00:04:11,546
Why you are doing it.

70
00:04:11,586 --> 00:04:12,599
How to do it.

71
00:04:12,639 --> 00:04:14,219
What you should see.

72
00:04:14,259 --> 00:04:16,811
What to try if you don't see it.

73
00:04:17,051 --> 00:04:19,619
And a quick question to reflect on.

74
00:04:19,659 --> 00:04:23,568
The why is the most important part, so don't
skip it!

75
00:04:24,967 --> 00:04:25,958
Ready to start?

76
00:04:25,998 --> 00:04:29,601
Open a terminal, clone the workshop, and
step inside.

77
00:04:29,641 --> 00:04:33,243
Run npm test, and you should see fourteen
tests pass.

78
00:04:33,283 --> 00:04:36,199
Run npm run smoke for a quick health check.

79
00:04:36,439 --> 00:04:39,925
Then run npm start, and open the address it
prints,

80
00:04:39,965 --> 00:04:42,690
on port three thousand, in your browser.

81
00:04:42,730 --> 00:04:46,147
You should see eight cards that all say
untriaged.

82
00:04:46,187 --> 00:04:48,566
And please, do not run npm install.

83
00:04:48,606 --> 00:04:50,848
This project needs nothing extra.

84
00:04:52,258 --> 00:04:54,907
One tip: be honest in your reports.

85
00:04:54,947 --> 00:04:57,212
If a check passed, say passed.

86
00:04:57,252 --> 00:04:59,132
If it failed, say failed.

87
00:04:59,172 --> 00:05:02,128
And if you did not run it, say not run.

88
00:05:02,168 --> 00:05:04,587
That is a perfectly good answer!

89
00:05:04,827 --> 00:05:08,062
Honest evidence is worth more than a green
checkmark.

90
00:05:09,467 --> 00:05:09,867
Stuck?

91
00:05:09,895 --> 00:05:11,712
Ask yourself three things.

92
00:05:11,752 --> 00:05:13,283
Did I restart the app?

93
00:05:13,323 --> 00:05:15,068
Am I in the right folder?

94
00:05:15,108 --> 00:05:17,567
And which Copilot setup am I using?

95
00:05:17,607 --> 00:05:18,424
Made a mess?

96
00:05:18,464 --> 00:05:22,566
Workshop commands save a backup first, so
you can restore.

97
00:05:22,606 --> 00:05:23,709
And still stuck?

98
00:05:23,949 --> 00:05:25,051
Raise your hand.

99
00:05:25,091 --> 00:05:27,908
Every helper in the room has been there.

100
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:30,068
That's it!

101
00:05:30,108 --> 00:05:33,864
You now know the story, the team, and the
plan.

102
00:05:33,904 --> 00:05:40,084
By the end of today, Sam will have an
answer, and you will know why it works.

103
00:05:40,124 --> 00:05:41,619
See you in lab one!
